blinky (1.11-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Rank suggested ports by transaction translator as well as depth. A
    full-speed device on a high-speed hub needs a translator; multi-TT hubs
    give each port its own, single-TT hubs share one, and root ports need
    none. Ports are labelled accordingly in doctor and recover.
  * Stop offering the SuperSpeed half of USB 3 hubs. A full-speed camera
    plugged into one enumerates on its USB 2.0 companion, which is listed
    separately with its real translator type.
  * Demote "the last fault was on this bus" from the first sort key to a
    tiebreaker. It had ranked a root port that had just worked below a
    single-TT hub on another bus, on the strength of an old fault that was
    caused by a cable.

 -- antair <antairdo@gmail.com>  Sat, 10 Oct 2026 21:10:00 +0000

blinky (1.10-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Add `blinky recover`, which works the problem instead of describing it.
    It tries each remedy in turn -- drain the endpoint, set the kernel
    quirks, power-cycle, move to a shallower port, change the cable, and
    only after asking, fresh batteries -- verifying with a handshake after
    every one and stopping the moment the camera answers, then downloading
    while the link is good.
  * Rungs that cannot help are skipped: a camera already on a root port is
    not told to move, and knobs already set are not set again.
  * Steps needing a person state exactly what to do and then wait for the
    camera to be unplugged and reconnected, rather than asking for a keypress.

 -- antair <antairdo@gmail.com>  Fri, 10 Oct 2026 18:40:00 +0000

blinky (1.9-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * The window can now turn automatic downloading on and off, controlling the
    same background service as `blinky watch`, and shows whether it is
    running and whether it starts at login.
  * The window and the service take turns on the camera. Both would claim
    interface 0 and one would fail with EBUSY, so pressing Download pauses
    the service and restarts it afterwards; closing the window mid-operation
    restarts it too, rather than leaving it stopped.
  * The window notices the camera being plugged in or unplugged by itself,
    so Refresh is rarely needed.
  * Fit the window to the screen. Its natural height exceeded a 800px-tall
    laptop panel, and the minimum size made it impossible to shrink; the
    panels now compress and the initial size is taken from the screen.

 -- antair <antairdo@gmail.com>  Fri, 10 Oct 2026 17:30:00 +0000

blinky (1.8-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Add `blinky watch`: wait for the camera and download the moment it
    appears, with no prompting. For hardware whose pictures sit in volatile
    memory behind a link that often needs several attempts, this is the sane
    default rather than a convenience -- a transfer that dies halfway
    resumes on the next plug, because duplicate detection skips what is
    already saved and every attempt gains ground.
  * Ship a systemd user unit, so it can run from login:
    systemctl --user enable --now blinky-watch
  * Report each result as a desktop notification where notify-send exists.

 -- antair <antairdo@gmail.com>  Fri, 10 Oct 2026 11:40:00 +0000

blinky (1.7.1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Warn that the camera's memory is volatile. Pictures live in 8 MB of
    SDRAM kept alive by the batteries: unplugging is harmless, but a flat or
    removed battery erases everything irrecoverably. doctor says so whenever
    the link is broken, since stranded pictures are then on a clock, and
    list says so whenever there is something on the camera to lose.

 -- antair <antairdo@gmail.com>  Fri, 25 Sep 2026 19:20:00 +0000

blinky (1.7-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * doctor now understands how far down a hub chain the camera is. It reads
    the failing port path out of the kernel log, counts the hubs between it
    and the controller, and says so: a full-speed device from 2002 has its
    traffic wrapped in split transactions by every high-speed hub on the way,
    and a chain of them is the commonest way to make it unreachable.
  * The free-port list is now ordered shallowest first, labelled with each
    port's depth, and truncated so it stops recommending ports that are
    several hubs down -- which was advice against itself.

 -- antair <antairdo@gmail.com>  Fri, 25 Sep 2026 18:10:00 +0000

blinky (1.6.1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Fix the erase confirmation sizing badly. It pinned its width with
    setFixedWidth while its height came from a word-wrapped label, so the
    content had no room to grow -- and on Wayland a frameless window is told
    its scale factor only after the first layout, so the size it settled on
    could be wrong once scaling arrived. It is now a proper QDialog subclass
    that sizes from its own layout, re-measures on show, and centres on the
    window rather than relying on a monkey-patched paintEvent.

 -- antair <antairdo@gmail.com>  Sat, 19 Sep 2026 12:40:00 +0000

blinky (1.6-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Choose which pictures to keep: --formats png,jpeg,bmp, at least one
    required, with checkboxes in the window. BMP is new.
  * Choose which videos to keep: --video-formats avi,gif, at least one
    required. Animated GIF output is new, and the window shows these
    checkboxes only when the camera actually holds a clip.
  * Fixed output tree: pictures at the top, the camera's bytes in raw/,
    clips in videos/ and their GIFs in videos/gifs/.
  * Raw files are always kept and are no longer a choice. They are the only
    copy of what the camera holds, the basis of duplicate detection, and what
    --delete-after verifies against; a decoded picture cannot stand in for
    them because its bytes bear no relation to the camera's.
  * Fix animated GIF writing, which failed because Pillow infers the format
    from the file extension and the temporary file ended in .part.

 -- antair <antairdo@gmail.com>  Sat, 19 Sep 2026 11:50:00 +0000

blinky (1.5-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Default clips to 5 fps, the rate the camera actually records at, rather
    than the 10 fps previously guessed. SiPix's manual says "approximately
    3-4 frames per second" for these clips and the Blink II specification
    lists 5 fps; 15 fps is the live PC-camera mode, not stored video. The
    old default played every clip at about twice real speed.

 -- antair <antairdo@gmail.com>  Sat, 19 Sep 2026 10:30:00 +0000

blinky (1.4-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Support video clips properly, now that the format is known. A clip is
    Motion JPEG -- whole JFIF frames end to end, padded to 8 bytes -- not an
    AVI, so the .avi name libgphoto2 gives them produces a file nothing will
    play. Frames are 320x120 and carry the same two-field interleave as a
    still, decoding to 320x240.
  * Clips now keep their raw bytes in raw/ like stills do, and gain a real
    MJPEG AVI built beside the pictures, with hdrl/movi/idx1 written by hand
    and no ffmpeg needed.
  * blinky decode rebuilds a video from a kept raw, instead of silently
    decoding only its first frame into a photograph.
  * Add --fps. The camera records no timing, so the rate is a choice; 10 is
    the default.
  * The window shows a clip's first frame as its thumbnail.

 -- antair <antairdo@gmail.com>  Sat, 19 Sep 2026 09:40:00 +0000

blinky (1.3-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Download to blink-pics inside the user's Pictures folder by default,
    found through XDG so a localised or relocated Pictures folder is honoured.
  * Put the raw camera data in a raw/ subfolder, so the main folder holds
    only things worth looking at. --no-raw-subfolder, or unticking the box in
    the window, keeps the old flat layout. Video clips stay with the pictures
    either way, the .avi being both the raw data and what you watch.
  * Duplicate detection and file numbering both look in the folder and its
    raw/ subfolder, so switching layouts does not cause a re-download or a
    name collision.

 -- antair <antairdo@gmail.com>  Sat, 19 Sep 2026 08:20:00 +0000

blinky (1.2-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Stop treating the camera's photo index as an identity. It restarts at
    zero after an erase, so image0000 on the camera and image0000 in the
    output folder are routinely different pictures. Downloads now number on
    from the highest file already in the folder; --naming camera restores the
    old behaviour with a numeric suffix on a clash.
  * blinky-gui: fix new photos appearing as already saved after the camera
    was erased and reshot. A row's badge and thumbnail came from whether a
    file of that *name* existed, so every new photo showed green with the old
    photo's picture. Refresh now fingerprints against the folder and only
    marks a row saved when that content really is there.
  * blinky-gui: fix downloads silently overwriting earlier photos of the same
    name. The window wrote straight to imageNNNN and never looked for a free
    name, so an erase-and-reshoot destroyed the previous set.
  * blinky-gui: wait for a running transfer when the window is closed rather
    than tearing the worker thread down under Qt's feet.

 -- antair <antairdo@gmail.com>  Sat, 19 Sep 2026 07:10:00 +0000

blinky (1.1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Add `blinky delete`: erase everything, or the newest N photos. The
    protocol offers nothing finer -- 0x12 erases all, 0x11 erases the last.
    Always confirms, and refuses when there is no terminal to confirm at.
  * Add `download --delete-after`, which erases the camera only once every
    photo is downloaded and re-read from disk byte for byte. Refused if
    anything failed or if --images selected only some of them.
  * Recognise photos already in the output folder by content rather than by
    name, using a 4 KB prefix read, so a renamed copy is not fetched again
    and a different photo reusing a filename does not clobber it.
  * Add --format png|jpeg|both and --jpeg-quality.
  * Report a USB stall as its own condition, with the only fix that works.
  * blinky-gui: options for format, duplicate skipping and erase-after, the
    last behind a confirmation and the shared safety check.
  * Add an application icon, installed into /usr/share/icons/hicolor at nine
    sizes and used for the window itself. Rendered from the same code that
    paints the window, so the two cannot drift.
  * blinky-gui: fix a window that could not be moved and title bar buttons
    that did nothing. Dragging now goes through startSystemMove, since
    Wayland does not let a client position itself; the traffic lights accept
    their own mouse press, without which Qt delivered the release to the
    title bar instead; and an Aqua corner grip resizes via
    startSystemResize. The green light and a double-click now zoom.

 -- antair <antairdo@gmail.com>  Fri, 18 Sep 2026 22:30:00 +0000

blinky (1.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Initial release.
  * Direct pyusb implementation of the SiPix Blink II vendor protocol.
  * doctor: five ordered diagnostic checks with plain-language fixes, a
    survey of free USB ports, and kernel-log correlation.
  * download: raw bytes fsynced before decoding, whole-image retries with
    backoff, endpoint draining between attempts.
  * PNG output written directly from decoded pixels; clips left as AVI.
  * convert: batch .pnm to PNG with pixel-identical verification.
  * Ships a udev rule so the camera is usable without root.

 -- antair <antairdo@gmail.com>  Fri, 18 Sep 2026 21:00:00 +0000
